---
const paths = [
  {
    name: 'Price, then volume',
    color: '#386882',
    points: '44,150 232,150 232,30',
    arrows: '133,144 141,150 133,156 M226,96 232,88 238,96',
    area: '+0.5',
  },
  {
    name: 'Volume, then price',
    color: '#b06646',
    points: '44,150 44,30 232,30',
    arrows: '38,96 44,88 50,96 M133,24 141,30 133,36',
    area: '−0.5',
  },
];
---

<section class="signature-explainer" aria-labelledby="capture-the-chaos">
  <div class="signature-heading">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">The idea behind the project</p>
      <h2 id="capture-the-chaos">
        Rough path signatures.
        <br /> <em>What are they?</em>
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="signature-introduction">
      <p class="signature-definition">
        A <strong>path signature</strong> turns a sequence of observations into
        numerical features. Its first levels describe total changes and the
        order in which variables move; higher levels capture richer
        interactions. We used these features to give trading agents a structured
        view of recent market history.
      </p>
      <p class="signature-history">
        <strong>From Brownian motion to data.</strong> The irregular motion of
        particles studied in physics is a classic example of a rough path.
        Signatures have mathematical roots in K.-T. Chen’s iterated integrals;
        Terry Lyons introduced rough path theory in 1998 to work with such
        irregular signals. Today, signatures also serve as features for machine
        learning.{' '}
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.03255">Historical background ↗</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <aside class="signature-story" aria-labelledby="signature-story-heading">
    <h3 id="signature-story-heading">What sparked my interest</h3>
    <div class="signature-story-copy">
      <p>
        My stochastic processes professor, Xin Guo, told me how she used
        signatures at Amazon to support supply-chain decisions during the war in
        Ukraine, helping save several billion dollars in a period of extreme
        uncertainty.
      </p>
      <p>
        This example made me want to explore their use in finance. Dealing with
        irregular paths is precisely the problem rough path theory was developed
        to address; signatures give us a structured way to describe them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <div class="signature-example-grid">
    <figure class="order-example">
      <div class="order-heading">
        <h3>Same start. Same finish. Different sequence.</h3>
        <p>Did price move first, or did volume arrive first?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="path-pair">
        {paths.map((path, index) => (
          <div class="path-panel" style={{ '--path-color': path.color }}>
            <h4>
              <span>{index === 0 ? 'A' : 'B'}</span> {path.name}
            </h4>
            <svg
              viewBox="0 0 280 200"
              role="img"
              aria-label={`${path.name}. Both coordinates start at zero and end at one; signed area ${path.area}.`}
            >
              <path
                d="M44 20 V150 H249"
                fill="none"
                stroke="#aaa99f"
                stroke-width="1"
              />
              <polygon points={path.points} fill={path.color} opacity="0.1" />
              <path
                d="M44 150 L232 30"
                stroke="#aaa99f"
                stroke-dasharray="4 5"
                fill="none"
              />
              <polyline
                points={path.points}
                fill="none"
                stroke={path.color}
                stroke-width="3.5"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
              />
              <path
                d={`M${path.arrows}`}
                fill="none"
                stroke={path.color}
                stroke-width="2.5"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
              />
              <circle cx="44" cy="150" r="4" fill={path.color} />
              <circle cx="232" cy="30" r="4" fill={path.color} />
              <g fill="#646c66" font-size="12" font-family="sans-serif">
                <text x="34" y="168">
                  0
                </text>
                <text x="229" y="168">
                  1
                </text>
                <text x="28" y="34">
                  1
                </text>
                <text x="140" y="192" text-anchor="middle">
                  Price change
                </text>
                <text
                  x="14"
                  y="89"
                  text-anchor="middle"
                  transform="rotate(-90 14 89)"
                >
                  Cumulative volume
                </text>
              </g>
            </svg>
            <div class="area-label">
              <span>Signed area</span>
              <strong>{path.area}</strong>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Total changes match. Reversing the order flips the signed area, a
        second-order signature quantity. Illustrative normalized paths.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="signature-math" aria-labelledby="signature-formula-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">The mathematics behind the picture</p>
      <h3 id="signature-formula-heading">A sequence of iterated integrals.</h3>
      <div class="signature-equations">
        <math
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"
          display="block"
          aria-label="The signature of X on zero to T is the sequence one, S at level one, S at level two, and so on."
        >
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub>
          <mo>(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo>
          <msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo>
          <msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>)</mo>
        </mrow>
      </math>
        <math
          class="iterated-integral"
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"
          display="block"
          aria-label="Each level k coordinate is the integral, over ordered times zero less than t one through t k less than T, of the ordered channel increments d X superscript i one at t one through d X superscript i k at t k."
        >
        <mrow>
          <msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <munder>
            <mo largeop="true">∫</mo>
            <mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow>
          </munder>
          <mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow>
          <mo>⋯</mo>
          <mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><msubsup><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow>
        </mrow>
      </math>
      </div>
      <dl class="signature-symbols">
        <div>
          <dt>
            <i>X</i>
          </dt>
          <dd>The market path: here, price change and cumulative volume.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <i>k</i>
          </dt>
          <dd>
            The signature level: total changes at level 1, ordered interactions
            at level 2 and beyond.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <div class="signature-levels">
        <div>
          <p class="level-label">Level 1 · total change</p>
          <math
            xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"
            display="block"
            aria-label="Level one in channel i is X at T minus X at zero."
          >
          <mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow>
        </math>
          <p>Both paths have the same first level: (1, 1).</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="level-label">Level 2 · order through signed area</p>
          <math
            xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"
            display="block"
            aria-label="The signed area is one half of the level two price-volume term minus the volume-price term."
          >
          <mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow>
        </math>
          <p>
            <i>P</i> = price change; <i>V</i> = cumulative volume. Reversing
            their order changes <i>A</i> from{' '}
            <span class="positive-area">+0.5</span> to{' '}
            <span class="negative-area">−0.5</span>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="signature-truncation">
        In practice, we truncate the series at depth 2 or 3 and use its{' '}
        <strong>log-signature</strong> as the compact feature representation.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <p class="signature-takeaway">
    <strong>The signature supplies the features.</strong> The RL agent learns
    how to use them, subject to trading costs and portfolio constraints.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="signature-rationale" aria-labelledby="why-use-signatures">
  <div class="rationale-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Capture the Chaos</p>
    <h2 id="why-use-signatures">Why use signatures?</h2>
    <p>
      The aim was to{' '}
      <strong>turn recent market history into useful ML inputs</strong>, then
      compare how D3QN, PPO and SAC learned to trade from them.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="rationale-details">
    <p>
      <strong>Preserve the order of events.</strong> Averages and volatility
      summarize a window but discard much of its ordering. Raw windows retain
      the sequence, leaving the neural network to learn its interactions.
      Signatures encode those interactions explicitly in a fixed-length vector:
      a useful inductive bias for learning from financial paths.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Use a compact representation.</strong> We used time-augmented{' '}
      <strong>log-signatures</strong>: time preserves temporal information,
      while the log-signature removes algebraic redundancy. A{' '}
      <strong>lead–lag</strong> construction can additionally expose volatility
      through areas between a path and its delayed copy, at the cost of extra
      dimensions; it was disabled in our baseline.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Keep the computation manageable.</strong> Ordinary signatures
      through depth 3 contain{' '}
      <strong>84 terms for 4 channels, versus 584 for 8</strong>. Feature counts
      grow exponentially with depth, so we kept depths at 2–3. This
      representation structures the history; whether it improves trading still
      requires evaluation.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
